// ============================================================================
// Config Resolution · SERVICE LOADER
//
// Turns a provider class NAMED IN A FILE into the two facts the rest of the engine needs:
// the type is instantiated, and its interface methods are entry points.
//
// This is the resolvable half of reflection. `ServiceLoader.load(X.class)` is a permanent
// blind spot because the lookup is dynamic — but the implementation's name is a literal in
// a file in the repository, so the instantiation IS statically known. Without this a
// provider looks like a dead root: nothing constructs it and nothing calls its methods,
// even though the platform does both.
//
// The parser hands a provider's name over in dotted form (a.b.Outer$Inner arrives as
// a.b.Outer.Inner), which is exactly a client type's qualifiedName, nested or not: one
// equi-join, no reconstruction of the binary name. The library side keeps the older
// package + simple join beside it, because a library IR may still carry the flattened
// nested form (a.b.Inner).
// java_type (14): 2 qualifiedName 14 hash
// ============================================================================

// svc_provider_resolves(Provider, Descriptor, TypeHash) — the client type a provider names.
svc_provider_resolves(p, d, t) :- svc_provider_class(p, d, qname),
    java_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// A provider may name a LIBRARY class — a library's own descriptor usually does. Resolved
// with provenance so the instantiation label can distinguish the two; entry points below
// stay CLIENT-only, because a library provider's methods are not client code to reach.
svc_provider_resolves_lib(p, d, t) :- svc_provider_class(p, d, qname),
    lib_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
svc_provider_resolves_lib(p, d, t) :- svc_provider(p, d, pkg, simple),
    pkg != "", simple != "",
    lib_type(_, cat(cat(pkg, "."), simple), _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
svc_provider_resolves_lib(p, d, t) :- svc_provider_class_lib(p, d, qname),
    lib_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
svc_provider_resolves_lib(p, d, t) :- svc_provider_lib(p, d, pkg, simple),
    pkg != "", simple != "",
    lib_type(_, cat(cat(pkg, "."), simple), _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
// A library descriptor naming a CLIENT class is the plugin shape: the dependency declares
// the service, the client implements it and registers it in the dependency's file.
svc_provider_resolves(p, d, t) :- svc_provider_class_lib(p, d, qname),
    java_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// ── The type is REGISTERED: the container instantiates it and calls back into it ─────
// cfg_registered_type already drives all three entry-point shapes in entry-points.dl —
// callbacks by name, "overrides a library supertype" (which is what implementing a service
// interface IS, so no name list is needed), and the constructor.
cfg_registered_type(t, "service_loader") :- svc_provider_resolves(_, _, t).

// ── The type is INSTANTIATED, with provenance ───────────────────────────────────────
// Reported, never used to NARROW dispatch: the instantiation set can only ever be a lower
// bound (containers, factories and reflection instantiate types this cannot see), so
// filtering virtual dispatch on it would delete must-have edges. A consumer that wants
// precision may subtract; the graph itself stays sound.
type_instantiated(t, "service_loader") :- svc_provider_resolves(_, _, t).
type_instantiated(t, "service_loader_lib") :- svc_provider_resolves_lib(_, _, t).
